# PirateBox: stable interface name for the external USB Wi-Fi AP adapter.
#
# LIVE-INSTALLED at /etc/udev/rules.d/ (confirmed 2026-09-05; installed
# manually during the ALFA migration, not by any deploy/setup script -
# this file is still the source of truth, but changes here require a
# manual re-copy + `udevadm control --reload-rules` to take effect live,
# same as any other root-owned system config in this project). An
# earlier revision of this comment claimed it was only staged, pending
# a future install step - that was accurate when written but is now
# stale; corrected here rather than left to mislead a future reader
# against live reality.
#
# Problem this solves: Linux assigns "wlan0"/"wlan1"/... in whatever
# order interfaces enumerate, which depends on USB plug order and boot
# timing - not something PirateBox should build its identity of "the
# external AP radio" on. See docs/EXTERNAL-AP-ARCHITECTURE-DESIGN.md
# "Stable ALFA identity" for the full reasoning, including why USB
# serial number was rejected (this adapter reports a generic/blank
# serial - "000000000" - confirmed live during the Hardware Validation
# Round, not a per-unit identifier).
#
# What this matches: the MediaTek MT7612U chipset (USB VID:PID
# 0e8d:7612) bound to the mainline mt76x2u driver - i.e. any adapter of
# this exact chipset attached to this Pi, not a specific physical unit.
# This is a deliberate choice, not an oversight: if this exact ALFA
# AWUS036ACM dies and is replaced with another unit of the same model,
# the replacement gets the same stable name automatically, with no
# operator/udev-rule update required - the chipset itself, not a serial
# number, is what PirateBox's radio-role logic (see
# docs/EXTERNAL-AP-ARCHITECTURE-DESIGN.md "Radio role model") actually
# cares about identifying. The tradeoff: this rule would equally rename
# ANY MT7612U-based adapter plugged into this Pi, including one that
# was never intended as the PirateBox AP - acceptable here because this
# is single-purpose deployed hardware, not a general-purpose desktop,
# and a second simultaneous MT7612U adapter is not a configuration this
# project supports.
#
# Why match on driver+VID:PID and not just VID:PID: MediaTek chipsets
# appear across many OEM products under multiple driver families;
# restricting to the specific mt76x2u kernel driver (which was
# confirmed bound live during hardware validation) avoids a coincidental
# rename of an unrelated MediaTek-chipset device that happens to share
# a USB ID range but uses a different driver.
#
# Corrected 2026-09-03 (first live install attempt, ALFA migration
# round): the original line below matched ATTRS{idVendor}/ATTRS{idProduct}
# and DRIVERS== as raw ancestor-walk conditions. Confirmed live via
# `udevadm info -a`/`udevadm test` that this can never match on this
# hardware: idVendor/idProduct live only on the USB *device* node
# (1-1.3), whose own DRIVERS attribute is the generic "usb" composite
# driver, not "mt76x2u" - the actual mt76x2u driver binds one level
# down, on the USB *interface* node (1-1.3:1.0), which has no
# idVendor/idProduct attribute of its own. udev's ATTRS{}/DRIVERS==
# matching requires every condition in one rule to be satisfied on the
# SAME single ancestor device - no ancestor here ever satisfies all
# three at once, so the original rule was a silent, permanent no-match
# (confirmed: reading the file succeeded, but no rule-application trace
# for it ever appeared in `udevadm test`, and a real replug left the
# adapter as wlan1, unrenamed - not a timing/reboot-vs-replug issue,
# structural). Fixed by matching the equivalent ENV{} properties
# instead, which udev already imports directly onto the net device
# itself via the earlier `usb_id` builtin (confirmed present via
# `udevadm test`: ID_USB_DRIVER=mt76x2u, ID_VENDOR_ID=0e8d,
# ID_MODEL_ID=7612) - no ancestor-walk needed, since these are already
# properties of the device the rule is renaming.
#
# Privacy note: this rule names a piece of the operator's OWN AP
# hardware for the PirateBox appliance's internal bookkeeping - it is
# not visitor/client identification. It never touches, stores, or
# reasons about the MAC address of any device that associates to the
# AP. That distinction matters and is kept: see this project's existing
# rule against persisting visitor MAC addresses (docs/
# DEVICE-MEMORY-DESIGN.md, tools/backup_piratebox_data.sh) - unaffected
# by this file.
#
# Live status correction (2026-09-05 incident investigation): this
# file's own header above used to claim the rule wasn't yet installed -
# that was stale. The rule was confirmed live-installed at
# /etc/udev/rules.d/ during the ALFA migration and has been correctly
# renaming the interface on every add event since, including through
# the 2026-09-05 disconnect/
# re-enumeration incident (confirmed: the interface came back named
# "pb-ap", not "wlan1" - the rename itself was never the problem; see
# docs/OPERATIONAL-DECISIONS.md's dated entry and
# etc/systemd/system/hostapd.service.d/override.conf for what was).
#
# REMOVED 2026-09-15 (root-caused, not just "found unreliable" - see
# docs/IMPLEMENTATION-ROADMAP.md's prior "documented only" entry for
# this gap and docs/OPERATIONAL-DECISIONS.md's dated entry for the full
# evidence): this rule used to also carry
# ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="hostapd.service" here, intended to make
# systemd-udevd queue a "start hostapd.service" job the moment this ADD
# rule finished applying. It never actually worked, on any real ALFA
# blip, including two independent ones today. Root cause, confirmed via
# `udevadm test`: renaming an interface via NAME= causes the kernel to
# emit a SEPARATE, subsequent "move" uevent once the rename is visible
# under its final name - `udevadm test --action=add` on this exact
# device correctly computes SYSTEMD_WANTS=hostapd.service, but
# `udevadm test --action=move` shows this rule (matched on
# ACTION=="add") never fires at all for that second event. Live
# evidence matched exactly: `udevadm info -q property` on the live,
# already-renamed pb-ap device showed no SYSTEMD_WANTS property at all,
# and `systemctl show 'sys-subsystem-net-devices-pb\x2dap.device' -p
# Wants` was empty - systemd's own dependency graph for the device unit
# that actually matters (the one under the FINAL "pb-ap" name) never
# saw the property, even though the ADD-event simulation alone looked
# correct. This is exactly the kind of drift the "battle-tested,
# inotify-backed .path unit" alternative already suggested in the
# roadmap avoids - implemented as
# etc/systemd/system/piratebox-hostapd-recovery.path, which watches
# /sys/class/net/pb-ap directly rather than depending on which specific
# udev action a given kernel rename sequence happens to fire. This
# rule's own job (matching the chipset, renaming to "pb-ap") is
# untouched - only the now-confirmed-dead SYSTEMD_WANTS clause is
# removed from the line below.
SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add", ENV{ID_USB_DRIVER}=="mt76x2u", ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID}=="0e8d", ENV{ID_MODEL_ID}=="7612", NAME="pb-ap"
